@page "/preview/terminal"

@* A development preview of a MAUI screen, not a back-office destination.

   The terminal is Restaurant.Mobile — Razor in an Android WebView. The same
   components render in a desktop browser, so this route stands them up at the
   artboard's 1440×900 and lets the screen be seen and iterated without an
   emulator on the machine. Nothing here is part of the product: the back office
   has no terminal, and this page never ships to a venue.

   Two things the preview must not lie about, because a desktop browser is not
   the device:

   · Hover. The kit declares every :hover rule inside one @@media (hover:hover)
     guard, so a pointer surface gets the lift and the Android terminal never
     does. That guard is correct and this browser satisfies it, which means the
     preview would show a lift the device cannot produce. The frame below
     retracts it, so what you see is what the terminal does: no transform, and
     press feedback as the ripple tint (§8).
   · Size. The frame is a fixed box at whichever device the switcher is set to,
     and it does not scale to the window. A scaled preview reads as a smaller
     terminal and hides how much a 190px tile actually is; scroll the frame
     instead. The switcher changes the box, never the zoom.

   The screen's own data behaves exactly as it does on the device: MenuDataSource
   reads the API and falls back to seed data when it cannot be reached, and says
   which of the two is on screen. *@

<PageTitle>Terminal preview</PageTitle>

<section class="panel tp">

    <header class="tp__head">
        <p class="tp__kicker">DEVELOPMENT PREVIEW &middot; MAUI SCREEN</p>
        <h1 class="tp__title">Terminal &middot; Order entry</h1>
        <p class="tp__note">
            The same <code>TerminalShell</code> and <code>OrderEntryScreen</code> components
            <code>Restaurant.Mobile</code> renders, stood up at the terminal's 1440&times;900 &mdash;
            or at any of the candidate form factors &mdash; so the screen can be worked on without
            an emulator or the hardware. This route is not a back-office destination and ships to no
            venue. Hover lifts are retracted inside the frame, because the terminal has no hover.
        </p>
    </header>

    @* ─── The device-size switcher ────────────────────────────────────────────
       Development tooling. It has no handbook specification and no product
       counterpart: nothing in Part II owns a size picker, and this one exists
       only because a large-tablet form factor has to be worked on without the
       hardware in the room. It still spends the kit — every colour, radius,
       type and spacing value below is a token — because a dev tool drawn in
       stray hex is a second palette on the same page.

       It changes one thing: the width and height of the box the shell stands
       in. It does not scale, zoom, or restyle anything inside that box, so the
       shell lays out at each size exactly as it would on a panel of that size.
       That works because Part II-A's handheld step is a container query on the
       shell's own inline size (`@@container upos-shell (max-width: 800px)`),
       not a viewport media query — resizing this frame exercises the same rule
       the device does, and the browser window is irrelevant to it.

       The branch readout under the frame is a mirror of that condition, not a
       readout of it: the badge sits outside the shell, so it cannot query the
       shell's container, and .tp__stage declares a container of its own at the
       same 800px threshold and exactly the shell's width. The two are kept
       honest by the fact that the stage is the shell's parent box — same width,
       same condition, same answer.

       The 800px step is an edge and the switcher shows it as one. The 10.1"
       tablet in portrait is 800px wide, the condition is `max-width: 800px`,
       and `max-width` is inclusive — so 800 lands on the HANDHELD side, and a
       tablet gets the degraded single-column phone layout. That is what the
       rule as written does; the presets say so rather than nudging the number. *@

    <div class="tp__tool">
        <div class="tp__tool-head">
            <p class="tp__tool-kicker">DEV TOOL &middot; NOT PRODUCT UI &middot; UNSPECIFIED</p>
            <p class="tp__tool-note">
                Resizes the frame below and nothing else &mdash; no scaling, no restyling. The
                handheld step is a container query on the shell&rsquo;s own width, so the shell
                lays out here as it would on a panel this size. The fixed 393&times;785 frame
                further down stays where it is, so two shells at two widths are still on the
                page at once.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tp__sizes" role="group" aria-label="Device size">
            @foreach (var preset in Presets)
            {
                <button type="button"
                        class="tp__size @(preset == _active ? "tp__size--on" : null)"
                        aria-pressed="@(preset == _active)"
                        @onclick="() => _active = preset">
                    <span class="tp__size-name">@preset.Name</span>
                    <span class="tp__size-dim">@preset.Width &times; @preset.Height</span>
                </button>
            }
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="tp__kicker">
        @_active.Name &middot; @_active.Width &times; @_active.Height &middot; @_active.Provenance
    </p>

    <div class="tp__frame">
        <div class="tp__stage" style="width:@(_active.Width)px">
            <div class="tp__device" style="height:@(_active.Height)px">
                <TerminalShell ActiveDestination="order">
                    <OrderEntryScreen CustomerName="Mobile User" TableId="1" />
                </TerminalShell>
            </div>

            @* Which branch the shell is actually in, stated in the shell's own
               terms. Both spans are in the markup and the container query hides
               one, so the text is selectable and greppable rather than living
               in a CSS content string. *@
            <p class="tp__branch">
                <span class="tp__branch-line tp__branch-line--wide">
                    <span class="tp__branch-tag tp__branch-tag--wide">WIDE</span>
                    <code>@@container upos-shell (max-width: 800px)</code> does not match at
                    @(_active.Width)px &mdash; the full three-pane order entry.
                </span>
                <span class="tp__branch-line tp__branch-line--handheld">
                    <span class="tp__branch-tag tp__branch-tag--handheld">HANDHELD</span>
                    <code>@@container upos-shell (max-width: 800px)</code> matches at
                    @(_active.Width)px &mdash; the provisional degraded single column.
                    @if (_active.Width == 800)
                    {
                        <strong>
                            800 is the step itself, and <code>max-width</code> is inclusive, so a
                            tablet this wide gets the phone layout.
                        </strong>
                    }
                </span>
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* The handheld step, stood up beside the design it degrades from.

       Both frames are on this page at once and both are correct at once, which
       is the whole reason the step is a container query on the shell rather
       than a media query on the viewport: the rule asks how wide the shell is,
       and these two shells are 1440px and 393px in the same browser window. A
       viewport rule would flip both frames together the moment the window
       narrowed, and would report the opposite of the truth about the device.

       393x785 is what the Datalogic Memor 20's WebView reports for its
       1080x2160 screen at 440dpi, now that MainActivity hides both of Android's
       system bars: the terminal owns the whole panel, so the WebView is the
       whole panel. The number has moved twice. It read 393x785 while the window
       drew edge to edge underneath the bars, and that 785 was a defect — the
       shell was measuring 72px of chrome it did not own and was clipped at both
       ends. It read 393x713 while the window was fitted to them — 2160 less a
       66px status bar and a 132px navigation bar is 1962 device pixels, which is
       713 CSS pixels — which was correct for a terminal that shares its screen
       with the host. It reads 393x785 again now, the same figure as the defect,
       reached by removing the bars rather than by ignoring them. It is a real
       measurement off a real device at every step, not a chosen number — and
       Part II-A · Handheld is provisional for exactly that reason: the device is
       one candidate and the fleet is not settled. *@
    <p class="tp__kicker">393 &times; 785 &middot; handheld &middot; provisional</p>

    <div class="tp__frame tp__frame--handheld">
        <div class="tp__device tp__device--handheld">
            <TerminalShell ActiveDestination="order">
                <OrderEntryScreen CustomerName="Mobile User" TableId="1" />
            </TerminalShell>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// One box the shell can be stood in. <c>Provenance</c> is where the two numbers came
    /// from, and it is part of the preset rather than a comment because a size nobody can
    /// trace is a size somebody invented.
    /// </summary>
    private sealed record DevicePreset(string Name, string Provenance, int Width, int Height);

    /// The candidate fleet, narrowest first. Part II-A's Handheld preamble names three
    /// candidates — "the counter unit, the 10.1" handheld and this 5.7" device" — and the
    /// surfaces table names the terminal's own target. Exactly one of these figures has been
    /// off a real device; the rest are the panel classes, and each one says which it is.
    private static readonly DevicePreset[] Presets =
    [
        // The only measured pair on the list. The Datalogic Memor 20's WebView reports
        // 393x785 CSS px for its 1080x2160 panel at 440dpi, with both Android system bars
        // hidden. Part II-A · Handheld records the measurement and the two figures it
        // replaced.
        new("Handheld · 5.7\"", "Datalogic Memor 20 · measured on the device", 393, 785),

        // A 10.1" Android tablet is 1280x800 CSS px whichever panel it ships: a 1920x1200
        // screen is ~224ppi, which Android buckets to hdpi and a dpr of 1.5, and a 1280x800
        // screen is ~149ppi, which is mdpi and a dpr of 1. Both land on the same viewport.
        // Portrait is that pair rotated — and 800 is the step, which is why this preset is
        // the one the branch readout has something extra to say about.
        new("10.1\" tablet · portrait", "1280×800 CSS class, rotated · panel class", 800, 1280),
        new("10.1\" tablet · landscape", "1920×1200 at dpr 1.5 · panel class", 1280, 800),

        // The spec. "1440×900 is the terminal", Part II-A's preamble, and the surfaces
        // table's "1440×900 tablet, landscape". Every artboard is drawn here.
        new("Terminal · spec", "Part II-A · the primary design", 1440, 900),

        // The counter unit is a candidate with no stated resolution anywhere in the
        // handbook, so this pair is the class rather than a spec: 15.6" POS units are FHD,
        // 1920x1080, and at ~141ppi that is mdpi and a dpr of 1, so the panel is the
        // viewport. Nothing has been on a bench. Treat it as the widest case worth
        // rehearsing, not as a target.
        new("Counter unit · 15.6\"", "1920×1080 FHD at dpr 1 · nominal, unverified", 1920, 1080),
    ];

    /// 1440x900 is the primary design, so the page opens there.
    private DevicePreset _active = Presets[3];
}
